robco-term (0.1.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The terminal will run on a slow line if you ask it to. Switch on
    `[serial]` and a local shell's output arrives at the baud rate you
    name, 19200 by default, which fills an 80 by 25 screen in about a
    second. It is the reading that is slowed and not the drawing, so a
    program blocks when the buffer between you fills, exactly as it would
    have behind a real wire, and `Ctrl`+`C` stops the output because it
    stops the program writing it. A screen copied or searched holds what
    the screen holds. A shell reached over SSH and a tmux window are not
    slowed. The settings window has a Serial tab for both keys.
  * A destination can be an IPv6 address. `--ssh 2001:db8::1` goes where
    it says, and the brackets carry a port the way `ssh` spells one,
    `--ssh [2001:db8::1]:2222`. Before this the last group of the address
    was read as a port number and what was left went to the resolver,
    which had never heard of it.
  * The glass slows down when nobody is watching it. Clicking away to
    another window halves the rate the CRT animates at, and half a minute
    without a keystroke halves it again; a keystroke or a click brings it
    straight back. The steps multiply whatever `effects_frame_skip` is
    set to, so a screen tuned to run fast still runs faster than a
    default one does. Set `unfocused_frame_skip` or `idle_frame_skip` to
    1 to switch either step off.
  * The bank's records are built once and kept, instead of being rebuilt
    from the pieces on every frame to draw the same picture.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 18:15:00 +1000

robco-term (0.1.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A click lands on the row it is over. The pointer measured the glass
    against the window rather than against the picture drawn on it, so a
    click near the top of the screen was right and one further down took
    a row above the one under the cursor, by more the lower it fell and
    by more the wider the screen margin was set.
  * The bank costs about a tenth less to keep on screen. It was
    rasterised and uploaded to the graphics card again on every frame,
    twenty times a second, to put back the pixels already there; it is
    now built again only when a channel changes or the window is
    resized.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 13:22:50 +1000

robco-term (0.1.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Every so often something drawn walks across the glass and leaves. A
    whale spouts, three ducks paddle past, the D51 runs through. It is
    not a screen saver: it comes while you are typing, a keystroke does
    not chase it away, and it is drawn over the terminal's picture rather
    than into it, so text scrolls behind one and a selection copied
    across one still gives back what the session wrote. Every piece is
    off any cell it touches within about a second.
  * The Critters tab settles whether they visit, how long on average
    between one and the next, and which of the eight are still in the
    cast. The interval is an average and not a period, so one is never
    due.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 16:02:19 +1000

robco-term (0.1.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The catalogue's wide 16-pixel face is GNU Unifont, under the SIL Open
    Font License, covering the whole printable Basic Multilingual Plane
    and the emoji planes above it. The Unscii 16 it replaces carried a
    GPL-derived file this package could not distribute under its own MIT
    terms.
  * A face whose catalogue row names a fallback now reaches it: the
    bundled faces answer an uncovered character before the machine's own
    fonts are read, so a bare container and a loaded desktop draw the
    same glyph.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 09:51:54 +1000

robco-term (0.1.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * PuTTY private keys work as they are: a [[ssh.host]] row may name a
    .ppk file (v2 or v3, encrypted or not), and an encrypted one asks
    for its passphrase on the glass like any other key.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Fri, 28 Aug 2026 10:15:23 +0000

robco-term (0.1.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * On macOS the settings window is reached the way a Mac reaches things:
    Ctrl+click as the right-click, and a Settings item on Cmd+, in the
    application menu the platform draws.
  * Cmd holds the unbroken copy of a wrapped selection on macOS, Ctrl
    being the secondary click there; Ctrl still holds it elsewhere.
  * Cmd+Q closes the terminal through its own shutdown, so the
    single-instance socket and lock are cleared rather than left behind.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Fri, 28 Aug 2026 09:29:43 +1000

robco-term (0.1.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The settings window opens on its own knowledge: the schema is data in
    the window's code, held to the terminal's --dump-settings by a test,
    and no process is run or parsed on the right-click.
  * The terminal names its own binary to the settings window it spawns,
    so the system-font list is asked of the terminal that will render it.
  * The TOML editing under the window is the tomledit 1.0 module from the
    teatotal shelf, vendored: an edit changes one key's bytes and no
    others.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Thu, 27 Aug 2026 23:12:13 +1000

robco-term (0.1.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The private key a [[ssh.host]] row names is offered on every window's
    connection, the startup default's included.
  * A PuTTY .ppk key file is refused by name, with the puttygen
    conversion spelled out on the glass, instead of a bare read error.
  * On a local screen, a new tab dials the configured default connection,
    the same way a fresh window does.
  * The font list is the bundled catalogue unless "Also offer installed
    system fonts" is ticked, and the machine's fonts are read only when a
    font picker is on screen. The settings window opens without the scan.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Thu, 27 Aug 2026 11:32:58 +0000

robco-term (0.1.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Built-in SSH grows its operator surface: passwords, key passphrases
    and keyboard-interactive answers are typed on the terminal's own
    glass, and an unknown host key asks yes/no before being recorded.
  * ~/.ssh/config is read whole; a directive the terminal cannot honour
    refuses the file by name rather than half-obeying it.
  * The mouse selection is drawn, in inverse video through the CRT chain.
  * Terminal queries (device attributes, cursor position and kin) are
    answered, and idle SSH connections send keepalives once a minute.

 -- Weiwu Zhang <a@colourful.land>  Thu, 27 Aug 2026 15:12:12 +1000


robco-term (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First Debian packaging: the terminal, its desktop entry and icon, and
    the Tcl/Tk settings window running on the distribution's tcl9.0/tk9.0.

